{%- comment -%}
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  Rosalia · Funnel — "The Daily Body" everyday-objects article
  ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
  Companion piece to Human Wiring, same reader, different angle: the
  ordinary objects a flusher meets before noon. Runs bare — the funnel
  suffix list in layout/theme.liquid strips the store header and footer.

  CHANGED FROM THE SUPPLIED DRAFT, AND WHY

  1. Disclosure at the top. The draft claimed to be independent with no
     paid placements and then recommended the publisher's own serum.
     That combination is what 16 CFR §465.5 calls a company-controlled
     review website. One honest line fixes it.

  2. The poll keeps its mechanic and loses its numbers. "41% of readers
     picked this one" is a made-up statistic about made-up readers. The
     tap now returns what that pattern MEANS — which is the useful half
     anyway, and it is all mechanism we can actually stand behind.

  3. The 19% / n=1,134 figure stays, because it is real: the National
     Rosacea Society workplace survey of 1,134 patients found 19% of
     respondents attributed a denied promotion to their rosacea.
     Verified before shipping.

  4. The "82% of 1,023 members" figure was removed — could not verify
     the survey, the year or the number, and a specific statistic
     attributed to a named organisation has to be checkable. The
     paragraph's argument does not need it: the reasoning about "gentle"
     being measured against a market average rather than against this
     reader's barrier stands on its own.

  5. Reader letters removed. Quotes signed "K., 46" and "A., 51" are
     testimonials from people who do not exist.
{%- endcomment -%}

